+
+### Methods
+
+
+A method is the named way a role does one job. `RIGGING.md` holds the project's methods, and they are
+its whole executable surface: a role runs a method, and the fit composes it. The word is always
+method, because a runtime's tools and a shell's commands are other things.
+
+Every job that needs the project's own tooling has one: the runner, the type checker, the linter,
+coverage, the plank join, static discovery, the package manager, the ship and its live check. A role
+reaches directly for what needs none of that, such as reading a file, asking git for the tree, or
+searching for a token. For a job in the table, the method IS the way: grepping for planks answers the
+plank-form question with an opinion, and the join answers it with a verdict.
+
+| Method | The job | Run by | Takes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `prove` | prove a named scenario set | Crew, Boatswain | `SS_SCENARIO` |
+| `verify` | verify a watch's whole target set and join its planks | QM | `SS_SCENARIO` |
+| `sweep` | enumerate one whole tier, reporting every failure it finds | QM, Boatswain | tier suffix |
+| `plank-join` | join every plank to a current step-definition pattern, and redden where the join fails | Shipwright | |
+| `hygiene` | answer every deck-hygiene check in one run | Boatswain | |
+| `static` | prove a non-executable hunk, a deletion, or configuration | Boatswain | |
+| `discovery` | prove the specs still bind to step definitions | Shipwright | |
+| `regression` | harbour's one full regression, every tier, cheapest first | Shipwright | |
+| `condemnation` | prove a condemnation removal batch | Shipwright | `SS_SCENARIO` |
+| `dead-code` | report what nothing reaches: unused files, exports and dependencies | Shipwright, Boatswain | |
+| `spec-lint` | lint Captain-authored specs and assets at write time | Captain | |
+| `install` | install or upgrade a dependency | Shipwright, and Captain at bootstrap | `SS_DEPENDENCY` |
+| `ship` | ship one outbound target | Captain | per target |
+| `ship-verify` | verify the live artifact that target's users consume | Captain | per target |
+
+Every method in the table gets its key. A job this project has nothing to do reads `none`, so a
+project with no outbound target still carries `ship` and `ship-verify`, and a role can tell a derived
+job from an absent one. Where a stack's runner has no form for a job, the method reads `none` and the
+fitting-out report names the gap, so the rigging stays honest about what this project can prove.
+
+The job is the fixed thing. A method does its job on this stack, reports each part separately, and
+returns each tool's own status so a red stays red. Doctrine's forms are the ones to write; where this
+project already runs a better tool for that job, the fit uses it and names the choice in its report.
+
+A method value is one Yoink plan, in backticks: one part per command the job runs, each part labelled
+and given the time it needs, the whole plan bounded. Yoink returns each part's output and each part's
+own status separately, which is what a job needs: the role judges the parts one at a time. The plan
+lives in `RIGGING.md` itself, so the project keeps one list of what its methods are, and a role runs
+the value as written. The Shipwright skill carries how each method is derived and proven.
+
+A method returns one multipart bundle, and a role reads it without needing any other skill installed.
+The bundle carries three sections per part, each introduced by the plan's boundary line: a JSON
+metadata object under `name="metadata"`, then `name="stdout"`, then `name="stderr"`. The metadata
+names the part, `label` and `command`, and reports its own result: `exitCode`, `signal`, `timedOut`,
+`durationMs`, and `stdout_truncated` with `stderr_truncated` where a part outran the plan's byte
+bound. So a step judges each part by its own label and its own status, and a truncated part says so.
+The parts appear in the plan's order, and the bundle ends with the boundary line followed by two
+dashes.
+
+A method's parts are independent: each runs whatever the others return. Where a job's next act
+depends on what the last one returned, that job is two methods run in turn. A method composes the
+acts its role already does, so `prove` proves what it is given.
+
+Methods are Shipwright's to derive and repair, on every project and at every stage: a greenfield repository is fitted out by Shipwright too, once Captain has installed the harness that gives it something to derive from. Captain MAY write a method when resolving a rigging blocker with the user, under the same write-scope exception. A role runs the
+method as `RIGGING.md` carries it, verbatim, with only the substitutions this contract names; where a
+step's method is missing, that is a configuration blocker to Captain, routed to Shipwright to refit,
+and the refit is what supplies it.
 
